Testing:
  Can interpreted patlang run the patlang native interpreter to run some code?
  do events work properly in the patlang interpreter?
  do events work properly in the patlang-native interpreter between processes (i.e. write at least two scripts to be interpreted, with at least one event handler in each and see that they can raise events which the other one handles correctly)?

Development:
  Can we define events inside a function/object (at the moment I think they are at a file level... but they ought to be within object (and everything is an object...)

  Can we look at an inheritance model for patlang (we discussed this, it is broadly the same as javascript protypes in this regard - would be good to be able to inherit even though we favour composition)

  We should consider a cuda kernel target for the patlang compiler; we could then write some cuda kernels in patlang, and have it compile to cuda kernels (and then have it run on the gpu?) Similar to Pythons Triton by OpenAI

  Examples update:

  A plain example for each of the paradigms (imperative, procedural, functional, OO, logic, event, goal oriented, DSL etc) 
  A simple example for each *pair* of paradigms together (can ignore 'imperative' and 'procedural' for this, as they are implicit in any other example code)
  A simple example for each *triple* of paradigms together (can ignore 'imperative' and 'procedural' for this, as they are implicit in any other example code)
  A simple example for each *quadruple* of paradigms together (can ignore 'imperative' and 'procedural' for this, as they are implicit in any other example code)
  All organised nice and cleanly with accompanying documentation for parslow site illustrating the pros and cons of the paradigms and the advantages of being able to use them together.
